<p>Web excursions brought to you in partnership with <a href="https://www.mindmeister.com/?r=116987">MindMeister</a>, the best collaborative mind mapping software out there.</p>

<dl>
  <dt><a href="https://tungstenedge.app/en/">Tungsten Edge</a></dt>
  <dd>Tungsten Edge is a per-window taskbar for macOS: every window in view, one click away. Precise switching, windows stay put; light, lean, and at one with macOS. Open source, macOS 12 or later.</dd>
  <dt><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/kolibri-move-windows/id6806594778?mt=12">‎Kolibri</a></dt>
  <dd>Moom does this, but it&rsquo;s one of my favorite features and if it&rsquo;s all you need: Kolibri lets you hold down a modifier key combo and move your mouse to move and resize windows without having to grab edges or toolbars. The App Store version uses Shortcuts, but once you buy it you can grab the <a href="https://kolibri.arvidson.tech/">Pro version</a> for free, which uses accessibility and offers a better experience.</dd>
  <dt><a href="https://www.usenet-rewind.com/">Usenet-Rewind</a></dt>
  <dd>Search Usenet news text-message archives from 1981 to present across hundreds of thousands of newsgroups.</dd>
  <dt><a href="https://birchfeed.app">Birchfeed</a></dt>
  <dd>A contender in the RSS market, for those of us who still use RSS. $29/year. I haven&rsquo;t fully explored it yet, so I don&rsquo;t know if it has some of the niceties of FeedBin (like newsletter subscription), which is what I currently use. Worth a look, though.</dd>
</dl>

